# `optim::audit_optima_derivatives`

Compares hand-written derivatives with automatic ones for the objective and the nonlinear constraints.

## Signature

```cpp
optim::DerivativeAuditReport optim::audit_optima_derivatives(const optim::ConstrainedProblem &problem, const optim::ParameterPack<pluss::Series> &params, const optim::DerivativeAuditOptions &options = {});
```

## What It Does

When a problem supplies both an analytic form and a traced form of the same function, the audit evaluates both at `params` and reports whether values and derivatives agree within `options.absolute_tolerance` (default `1e-8`) and `options.relative_tolerance` (default `1e-6`). The report has an entry for the objective, the nonlinear equalities and the nonlinear inequalities, each with `available`, `passed`, the differences and a message, plus an overall `passed`. An entry whose two forms are not both present is `available = false`. Run the audit once when you write an analytic gradient or Jacobian: a wrong derivative otherwise shows up only as a solver that fails to converge.

## Parameters

`problem`
: A problem with analytic and traced forms.

`params`
: The point to compare at.

`options`
: Absolute and relative tolerances.

## Returns

A `DerivativeAuditReport`.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

A correct and a wrong analytic gradient.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace optim = pluss::subsp::math::optim;
using Pack = optim::ParameterPack<pluss::Series>;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const Pack at = optim::pack_series(pluss::series::make({1.0, 2.0}));
  const auto make = [](double scale) {
    return optim::problem().objective_analytic([scale](const Pack &p) { return std::pair{p.at("x").square().sum(), optim::pack_series(scale * p.at("x"))}; })
        .objective([](const Pack &p) { return p.at("x").square().sum(); }).build();
  };
  const optim::DerivativeAuditReport good = optim::audit_optima_derivatives(make(2.0), at);
  const optim::DerivativeAuditReport bad = optim::audit_optima_derivatives(make(3.0), at);
  out.writeln("correct: passed =", good.passed, ", derivative difference =", good.objective.derivative_abs_diff);
  out.writeln("wrong  : passed =", bad.passed, ", derivative difference =", bad.objective.derivative_abs_diff);
}
```

### Example 2

Nothing to compare. A problem with only a traced objective has no analytic form, so the entry is unavailable.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace optim = pluss::subsp::math::optim;
using Pack = optim::ParameterPack<pluss::Series>;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const pluss::Series target = pluss::series::make({1.0, 2.0});
  const optim::ConstrainedProblem problem =
      optim::problem()
          .objective([&](const Pack &p) { return (p.at("x") - target).square().sum(); })
          .nl_ineq([](const Pack &p) { return p.at("x").square().sum() - 1.0; });

  const optim::DerivativeAuditReport report = optim::audit_optima_derivatives(problem, optim::pack_series(pluss::series::make({0.0, 0.0})));
  out.writeln("objective available =", report.objective.available, ", message =", report.objective.message);
}
```

### Example 3

Looser tolerances admit a small error.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace optim = pluss::subsp::math::optim;
using Pack = optim::ParameterPack<pluss::Series>;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const Pack at = optim::pack_series(pluss::series::make({1.0, 2.0}));
  const optim::ConstrainedProblem nearly = optim::problem().objective_analytic([](const Pack &p) { return std::pair{p.at("x").square().sum(), optim::pack_series(2.0000001 * p.at("x"))}; })
      .objective([](const Pack &p) { return p.at("x").square().sum(); }).build();
  optim::DerivativeAuditOptions loose;
  loose.absolute_tolerance = 1e-5;
  out.writeln("default tolerance passes =", optim::audit_optima_derivatives(nearly, at).passed);
  out.writeln("loose   tolerance passes =", optim::audit_optima_derivatives(nearly, at, loose).passed);
}
```

